Instruction: Read the following segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by selecting the option that best characterizes the agreement.

Question: Is the “ability to consummate” concept subject to Material Adverse Effect (MAE) carveouts?
Option A: No
Option B: Yes

Merger Agreement: 
“For purposes of this Agreement, the term ‘Material Adverse Effect’ means any change, event, or circumstance, whether occurring individually or in aggregation, that has, or would reasonably be expected to have, a material adverse impact on (a) the business, assets, liabilities, financial condition, and results of operations of the Company, or (b) the ability of the Company to consummate the transactions contemplated herein. Notwithstanding the foregoing, the following shall be excluded from the determination of a Material Adverse Effect:

(i) Any general market fluctuation or macroeconomic change that impacts similar companies in the industry;
(ii) Changes solely attributable to modifications in GAAP, regulatory accounting standards or broadly applicable legal reforms;
(iii) Natural disasters, political upheavals, or other force majeure events that affect the industry at large;
(iv) Any event that results in a delay in consummation of the transaction without adversely affecting the overall financial viability or operational strength of the Company.

However, if an event simultaneously undermines the Company’s financial performance and impairs its operational capability to consummate the transaction in a timely manner, such effect shall not be excluded.

This Agreement expressly contemplates that certain delays in consummation, when isolated from material financial impact, are carved out of the definition of a Material Adverse Effect.
