Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.
Question: Is the “ability to consummate” aspect of the transaction subject to Material Adverse Effect carveouts?
Option A: No
Option B: Yes

Merger Agreement: "Acquisition Material Harm" shall mean any event, change, occurrence or condition that, either individually or in aggregate with all other similar events, either (a) has a material adverse effect on the general operations, financial condition or prospects of Party X or (b) would reasonably be expected to prevent, materially delay or impair Party X’s ability to consummate the Transaction. Notwithstanding the foregoing, none of the following shall be deemed to constitute an Acquisition Material Harm:
   (i) fluctuations in currency exchange or interest rates provided such fluctuations do not exceed industry-standard thresholds;
   (ii) general changes in regulatory or legislative conditions affecting all market participants, including modifications pursuant to non-targeted federal or state laws;
   (iii) force majeure events such as hurricanes, floods or other natural disasters, except where such events have a uniquely severe and disproportionate impact on Party X relative to its peers;
   (iv) routine adjustments in accounting practices in line with internationally accepted GAAP.
Furthermore, the provision explicitly states that any event preventing Party X from consummating the Transaction shall be considered material, except where the event falls within carveouts (i) through (iv) as set forth above.
